# Sentinel — Architecture Overview

Status: High‑level architecture reference

This document provides a quick mental model of Sentinel’s architecture. It is intentionally brief and does not repeat detailed design decisions, which are covered in the system design document.

---

## Purpose of this document

This exists to answer one question:

“How is Sentinel structured, and where does responsibility live?”

If you want implementation details, edge cases, or rationale, refer to `system-design.md`.

---

## Architectural principle

Sentinel treats the language model as a dependency, not the system.

All responsibility for safety, trust, memory, and governance lives outside the model, in deterministic backend logic.

---

## High‑level flow

User
→ Frontend (UI only)
→ Backend API
→ Confidence & safety logic
→ Memory & state
→ Logs & monitoring
→ External LLM API

The response follows the same path back to the user, with confidence signals attached by the backend.

---

## Component breakdown

### Frontend

Role:

* Collect user input
* Display responses
* Display confidence state (confident / guessing)

Constraints:

* No business logic
* No safety logic
* No memory decisions

The frontend is intentionally thin.

---

### Backend API

Role:

* Owns system behavior
* Assembles context
* Evaluates confidence
* Enforces safety and governance rules
* Controls memory usage
* Produces structured logs

This is where Sentinel actually lives.

---

### Confidence & Safety Layer

Role:

* Evaluate model outputs
* Detect uncertainty, inconsistency, and overconfidence
* Decide when to ask clarifying questions
* Decide when to suggest external verification

This layer is deterministic and does not negotiate with UX.

---

### Memory & State

Role:

* Maintain short‑term session context
* Store long‑term topic‑level signals (with consent)

Constraints:

* No raw message storage
* No personal or emotional data
* Manual reset only

---

### Logs & Monitoring

Role:

* Capture every interaction
* Enable audits and debugging
* Track long‑term behavioral patterns

Logs are append‑only and treated as a source of truth.

---

### External LLM API

Role:

* Generate text

Constraints:

* Stateless
* Replaceable
* Never trusted blindly

The model does not know users, memory, or safety rules.

---

## Responsibility boundaries

* The model generates text
* The backend decides whether that text should be trusted
* The system, not the user, owns safety

This separation is the core architectural decision behind Sentinel.

